#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449 :topic/math-academy-id 1931 :topic/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050675 :topic/math-academy-id 303 :topic/title "Differentiating Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "b6ff39e3-80ec-5a87-8943-dd2c886031a8" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124639 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124197 :question/id #uuid "35a664b8-a895-5c52-a92f-b62c75b44744" :question/math-academy-id "e-11026" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ given that $f(x,y) = 3x\\ln y + 2ye^x.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =3x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+2{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}+2{e}^{x}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{y}+2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Two-Variable Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124643 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124201 :question/id #uuid "a14d53ae-1922-531d-bb2a-bda9b4829f6e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11045" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y) = y^2\\ln{x},$ express $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ as the limit of a difference quotient." :question/worked-solution "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\left(\\dfrac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x) = y^2 \\ln{x},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y) = y^2\\ln(x+h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0}\\left( \\dfrac{y^2\\ln(x+h) -y^2\\ln{x} }{h}\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Partial Derivatives as Limits of Difference Quotients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124202 :question/id #uuid "ee2294e1-1e9a-5be5-be47-4c2e295b640a" :question/math-academy-id "e-11049" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) = x^2y^2+\\sin{x}\\cos{y},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,2\\pi )} & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot (2\\pi )^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4{\\pi }^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8{\\pi }^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Partial Derivative at a Point"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1929 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048630 :topic/math-academy-id 4096 :topic/title "Computing Partial Derivatives Using the Rules of Differentiation"}] :topic/title "Introduction to Partial Derivatives"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048783 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297 :topic/math-academy-id 147 :topic/title "Multiplying Square Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825 :topic/math-academy-id 1195 :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column Vector"}] :topic/id #uuid "be557898-2dc0-5c08-b4e7-63bbb4f08f56" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100691 :question/id #uuid "da92ef6c-cb0b-5a4c-9a0d-0f0a9db19616" :question/math-academy-id "e-4403" :question/problem "Let\n![](images/53/5357fcb0ec150699b4d50cbd6fa9651c421ac3500660703396146362160328a2.png)\nand\n![](images/63/63d354773d145f05e73e808efe2a21164cb28d6ff19ae104d6bbde6510cf10e6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product $A\\times B$ is well defined\n\n- The product $B\\times A$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, and $B$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{2 \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{2}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{2}} \\times 3}{B} = \n\t{\\color{green}\\text{well defined}}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $B$ and $2$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{2 \\times \\color{red}{3}}{B} \\underset{\\color{red}\\neq}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{red}2} \\times 2}{A} = {\\color{red}\\text{undefined}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, while statement II is false."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100870 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100692 :question/id #uuid "c677ee1a-d3c2-534c-985c-efaa5dc04c86" :question/math-academy-id "e-4404" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $3 \\times 4$ matrix and $B$ is a $4 \\times 2$ matrix. What are the dimensions of $AB$?" :question/worked-solution "There are $4$ columns in $A$ and $4$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication, and $AB$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{4}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{4}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{B} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{AB}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Product of Two Matrices Given the Dimensions of the Factors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100871 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100693 :question/id #uuid "9352349d-64e0-5437-9d72-7e7f7e571064" :question/math-academy-id "e-4405" :question/problem "Let\n![](images/03/035755bae569c8c9323112891f959076dd727d3ef40ba66c6c40dadf5ddc8f84.png)\nand\n![](images/e5/e53c9ba3bb64b81e44f8f4f49ac77408b7db6ee458be3d43b687d9ed8897ad59.png)\nWhat are the dimensions of $BA?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that $A$ is a $4 \\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2 \\times 4$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $B$ and $4$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $BA$ has dimensions $2 \\times 2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{4}}}{B} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{4}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{A} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{BA}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Product of Two Matrices"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1198 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407 :topic/math-academy-id 1196 :topic/title "Multiplying Matrices"}] :topic/title "Conformability for Matrix Multiplication"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048783 :topic/math-academy-id 1198 :topic/title "Conformability for Matrix Multiplication"}] :topic/id #uuid "4681afbb-a1dd-511c-bbd0-1eabc66634e9" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100872 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100694 :question/id #uuid "66ec04a0-2af8-5187-98b2-b06eedd5b0a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4406" :question/problem "Find $CD$, if \n![](images/1a/1a753977affc5cc3dda18c9206116e1132254bccebd1e3f2545bea75bcc38f18.png)\nand \n![](images/b9/b99016bca94adc5de11eddef950fa13fc4349aaf95387468d07f294c409d029d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Here, $C$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix and $D$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $C$ and $3$ rows in $D,$ so $C$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{3}}}{C} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{D} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{CD}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $CD,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=1\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-1=2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,4=2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/64/64b831a66cdd9a3176d1eb916dfae189b41eb1f0f3ed3184797f92e404957878.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Two Matrices When They Are Conformable"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100695 :question/id #uuid "3a696ed3-e341-5ea6-bf17-681ba8e386c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4407" :question/problem "Let $C=AB.$ What is the value of $c_{32}$, if\n![](images/c6/c6c7e580cfd96bc23c55742535ceb40a94635c2a44b911e0d08311c3a599c2fa.png)" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $3$rd row in $A$ by the $2$nd column in $B$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of the Product of Two Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100741 :question/id #uuid "c3ed8d05-758d-5ae0-bf57-3779fde6020b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5885" :question/problem "Compute the product \n $\\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n       -1 \\\n \t0 \\\n        -2\n \t\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        2 & -5\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, the first factor is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} {\\color{green}\\checkmark} {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} = \n\t\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n       -1 \\\n \t0 \\\n        -2\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        2 & -5\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        (-1)\\cdot 2 & (-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\n        0\\cdot 2 & 0\\cdot (-5)\\\n        (-2)\\cdot 2 & (-2)\\cdot (-5)\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack \\[5pt]\n\n\t&=\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n       -2 & 5 \\\n       0 &  0 \\\n       -4 &  10 \n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack \n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Column-Vector By a Row-Vector"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1196 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048122 :topic/math-academy-id 5916 :topic/title "The Hadamard Product"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048275 :topic/math-academy-id 2015 :topic/title "Matrices Over the Complex Numbers"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049420 :topic/math-academy-id 866 :topic/title "Linear Transformations of Objects in the Plane"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049970 :topic/math-academy-id 2124 :topic/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Arbitrary Bases)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050122 :topic/math-academy-id 1724 :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050324 :topic/math-academy-id 2014 :topic/title "Connections Between Matrix Representations of a Linear Transformation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050911 :topic/math-academy-id 3138 :topic/title "The Sample Covariance Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050972 :topic/math-academy-id 3121 :topic/title "Bilinear Forms"}] :topic/title "Multiplying Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048245 :topic/math-academy-id 3770 :topic/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489 :topic/math-academy-id 478 :topic/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :topic/id #uuid "7280f087-059d-5524-8f8b-a1fe79b4ef9d" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064099 :question/id #uuid "60a223c7-3d1c-50d6-be7c-330ba039635c" :question/math-academy-id "e-491" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv' \\, \\textrm{d}x= uv - \\int vu' \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle  \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle  \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{7x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}-\\int \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}\\int {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}(\\frac{{e}^{7x}}{7})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{49}{e}^{7x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Indefinite Integral Containing an Exponential Expression"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064100 :question/id #uuid "804f45cd-2082-5521-b4db-a32169716626" :question/math-academy-id "e-492" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x \\cos{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x \\cos{2x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Indefinite Integral Containing a Trigonometric Expression"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064117 :question/id #uuid "aaeb0407-771b-5dbc-8a50-e7bc0e3477c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6209" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/5} x\\sin(5x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+{\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)]+[\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi }{25}\\cdot (-1)+0]+[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Definite Integral Using Integration by Parts"}] :topic/math-academy-id 317 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169 :topic/math-academy-id 2096 :topic/title "Inner Product Spaces"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048569 :topic/math-academy-id 877 :topic/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048685 :topic/math-academy-id 3401 :topic/title "Moment-Generating Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049050 :topic/math-academy-id 6372 :topic/title "Laplace Transforms of Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049374 :topic/math-academy-id 3275 :topic/title "Mean and Variance of the Exponential Distribution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433 :topic/math-academy-id 3289 :topic/title "The Gamma Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050289 :topic/math-academy-id 2107 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/math-academy-id 3187 :topic/title "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050521 :topic/math-academy-id 1140 :topic/title "Using Integration by Parts to Calculate Integrals With Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050787 :topic/math-academy-id 1043 :topic/title "Calculating Areas Bounded by Parametric Functions"}] :topic/title "Introduction to Integration by Parts"}]}
